{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789849537,"nonce":"ZrXG9nYd5/MMps8EstuuLdvhxQ8=","title":"a `++`/`--` on the line after a block, `if`, `switch` or loop was parsed as a postfix on that block","message":"Both parsers continued any expression with a postfix `++`/`--`, so\n\n    switch (c) { ... }\n    ++p;\n\nbecame `(switch ...)++` followed by a bare `p`: the increment landed on the switch value and `p` never moved. Every `while` whose body ends a `switch` or `if` statement with a separate `++i` looped forever (haxe.xml.Parser.doParse read `p=0` on every iteration). Haxe ends the expression after a block, `if`, `for`, `while`, `switch`, `try` or function literal (grammar.ml does not call `expr_next` for those), so the operator starts the next statement; a parenthesised expression, an identifier, a field or an index still takes it (`(e[3])++`, `this++`).","files":null}]}